<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02009</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b7f7d3c0-39c6-400a-ab8d-8338efcf3183</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here are two sets of equations for quadratic functions you saw earlier. In each set, the expressions that define the output are equivalent. In other words, all the equations in Set 1 define the same function. The same is true for Set 2: all the equations define the same function but they are just written in different forms.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <emphasis>Set 1:</emphasis>
      </para>
      <para id="para-00003"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00004"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>g</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00005"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>h</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00006">
        <emphasis>Set 2:</emphasis>
      </para>
      <para id="para-00007"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>p</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>5</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00008"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>q</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00009"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>r</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mi>−</m:mi>
            <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00010">The expression that defines <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>h</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">vertex form</term>. We can show that it is equivalent to the expression defining <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> by expanding and simplifying the expression:</para>
      <para id="para-00011"><m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mtable>
              <m:mtr>
                <m:mtd columnalign="right">
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mtd>
                <m:mtd columnalign="center">
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                </m:mtd>
                <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mtd>
              </m:mtr>
              <m:mtr>
                <m:mtd columnalign="right" />
                <m:mtd columnalign="center">
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                </m:mtd>
                <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mtd>
              </m:mtr>
              <m:mtr>
                <m:mtd columnalign="right" />
                <m:mtd columnalign="center">
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                </m:mtd>
                <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mspace width="0.278em" />
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi mathvariant="normal">w</m:mi>
                    <m:mi mathvariant="normal">h</m:mi>
                    <m:mi mathvariant="normal">i</m:mi>
                    <m:mi mathvariant="normal">c</m:mi>
                    <m:mi mathvariant="normal">h</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mspace width="0.278em" />
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi mathvariant="normal">i</m:mi>
                    <m:mi mathvariant="normal">s</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mspace width="0.278em" />
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mtd>
              </m:mtr>
            </m:mtable>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00012">If this simplified expression is factored, then we arrive at the expression that defines function <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>g</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>: <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>4</m:mn>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>. The expression that defines <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>g</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">factored form</term>. The simplified expression that defines function <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">standard form</term>.</para>

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00013">1. Show that the expressions defining <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>r</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>p</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> are equivalent.</para>
          <para id="para-00014"></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00016">Expanding and simplifying function <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>r</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mi>i</m:mi>
                <m:mi>v</m:mi>
                <m:mi>e</m:mi>
                <m:mi>s</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, which equals <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <para id="para-00017">For questions 2 – 3, examine the graphs representing the quadratic functions.</para>
      <media alt="...">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_17_03_02.svg" width="268" />
      </media>

      <exercise id="ffff5156-2bf7-4567-96b2-7aa7f7191d18" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">

          <para id="para-00018">2. Examine the graph of the function <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">a. What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00020">Correct! -2</para>
          <para id="para-00021">The correct answer is -2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_467b3ca3-8550-4222-ba3f-a1c76865ea1b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">

          <para id="para-00022">b. What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00023">Correct! -4</para>
          <para id="para-00024">The correct answer is -4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_0773894f-eec9-475a-82ba-23df40c73c4f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">

          <para id="para-00025">3. Examine the graph of the function <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>r</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
            <media alt="...">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_17_03_03.svg" width="268" />
            </media>
          <para id="para-00026">a. What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>r</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00027">Correct! 3</para>
          <para id="para-00028">The correct answer is 3.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="abdf1679-d62b-4b66-bdb8-979b6c361039" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">

          <para id="para-00029">b. What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>r</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00030">Correct! 4</para>
          <para id="para-00031">The correct answer is 4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00032">4. Why do you think expressions such as those defining <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>r</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> are said to be written in vertex form?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00034">The numbers in vertex form seem to give the coordinates of the
            vertex of the graph. When a positive number is added to the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> in the parentheses, the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex seems to be negative—which is the opposite of that number. When a number is subtracted from <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex seems to be positive—which is the opposite of what it looks like.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Why Should I Care?</title>
          <media alt="A hand holds a tablet displaying a diagram of projectile motion: a cannon fires a ball at angle theta, following a curved path; the horizontal distance the cannonball travels is represented by s and labeled at the bottom of the trajectory. The height of the cannonball from the ground is represented by the variable h and labeled with a vertical line under the path.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_17_03_01.png" width="600" />
        </media>
        
        <para id="para-00035">When you launch an object, it does not travel in a straight line. Instead, it follows a curved path called a parabola. This means you have to account for distance, height, and angle if you want to hit your target. You can use quadratic equations to find these measurements.</para>
        <para id="para-00036">The next time you throw a ball, think about the parabolic path that it follows.</para>
      </note>
    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00002">
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00037"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_7_15_2_SC" /></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>


    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>
        <para id="para-00038">Quadratics can be written in different equivalent forms:</para>
        <list id="list-00001">
          <item>Standard form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item>Factored form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
          <item>Vertex form: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>k</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00039">Example</para>
        <para id="para-00040">Write <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> in standard and factored forms.</para>
        <para id="para-00041">Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>
        <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials.</para>
        <para id="para-00056"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials.</para>
        <para id="para-00057"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>9</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.</para>
        <para id="para-00058"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00045">Here is how to find the factored form:</para>
        <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - From the standard form, find the factors of the trinomial expression <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Examine the trinomial expression.</para>
        <para id="para-00059"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial.</para>
        <para id="para-00060"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00049">The factored form is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00003">
          <title>Try It: Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>
      
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
            <problem id="prob-00007">

              <para id="para-00050">Find the equation in standard form that is equivalent to <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.</para>

              <para id="para-00051"></para>

            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00007">
              <para id="para-00052">Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>

              <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials. <newline/><m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>

              <para id="para-00054"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials. <newline/><m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>

              <para id="para-00055"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms. <newline/><m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>

            </solution>
          </exercise></note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>